      "text": "Let's denote the two numbers by \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) with \\( x > y \\). According to the problem, we have two equations:\n\n1. The sum of the two numbers: \n\\[ x + y = 6 \\quad \\text{(Equation 1)} \\]\n\n2. The difference of their squares: \n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\quad \\text{(Equation 2)} \\]\n\nWe can use the identity \\( x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\) to simplify Equation 2. Substituting Equation 1 into this identity, we get:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\]\n\\[ 12 = 6(x - y) \\]\n\nDividing both sides by 6, we obtain:\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\n\nSo, the positive difference of the two numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this result by solving the system of equations numerically. We will use Python to ensure the calculations are accurate.\n```python\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\r\n\r\n# Define the symbols\r\nx, y = symbols('x y')\r\n\r\n# Define the equations\r\neq1 = Eq(x + y, 6)\r\neq2 = Eq(x**2 - y**2, 12)\r\n\r\n# Solve the system of equations\r\nsolutions = solve((eq1, eq2), (x, y))\r\nprint(solutions)\n```",
