      "text": "Let the two numbers be \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). From the problem, we have the following two equations:\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = 12 \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), we can use the identity for the difference of squares:\n\\[ x^2 - y^2 = (x + y)(x - y) \\]\n\nSubstituting the first equation into this identity, we get:\n\\[ (x + y)(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\\[ 6(x - y) = 12 \\]\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[ x + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ x - y = 2 \\]\n\nWe can solve this system by adding the two equations:\n\\[ (x + y) + (x - y) = 6 + 2 \\]\n\\[ 2x = 8 \\]\n\\[ x = 4 \\]\n\nSubstituting \\( x = 4 \\) back into the first equation:\n\\[ 4 + y = 6 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the two numbers are \\( 4 \\) and \\( 2 \\). The positive difference of these numbers is:\n\\[ |4 - 2| = 2 \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{2} \\]",
